99025 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186099026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099025 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "468f70d4-e1fe-51a6-a43a-7fc0681f374d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099026 #edb/ref 1080 "-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099026 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099026 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8d9063ef-6952-5b4a-b585-5b15a5a0b4c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099027 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099027 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099027 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "29beae2b-e3ad-54c1-b38c-77047fe5ae6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099028 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099028 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099028 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f07a31e-afd5-574c-b84a-4c775953dcd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099029 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099029 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099029 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c282323-a32d-5969-83ed-d938a4ad4bd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099030 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099030 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186099030 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7f9ab6ce-37b7-526f-92ce-0279e52b7b3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100581 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100581 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3e/3e9a879522107eb2b2381a688d1173fdf6a93a8b81686fd2f21c815008ec35df.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "caa282bb-e637-522c-a2da-b384b1ff855a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100581 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and $f'(x)\\neq 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =3 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+(-10) \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{3,-7\\} = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100698 #edb/ref 1069 "e-442" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100698 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $1 \\leq x \\leq 5.$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below. Find a value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-2.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4327bc76ae23907fb628cc9e9d5d045ab773070f9ce8fc2972dcf78e434b24d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100698 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be6b45d4-9257-5a84-8e19-50a315871d3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100698 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,2,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/7f/7f129ef5fe5d219f1bf57425682cea13ff657b9a539dc7ae0b2d172bf37c2c28.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(4) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}-1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} =  \\max\\left\\lbrace -2,-\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{3}{2},-1 \\right\\rbrace = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100699 #edb/ref 1069 "e-443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100699 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,6].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,6]$ given that $f(0)=1.$\n\n![](images/7c/7ca8e07d3438890b4f1e220e491d4a20f35c7473a31e76426b9a1fa13ed6926c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9fbd4cac-e923-57fd-94f3-79e323195d1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100699 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,6),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,4,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ f(6) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6-2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{1, 7, 5\\} = 7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=7,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100857 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186511459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218610085