the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=9,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101284 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101284 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101284 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101284 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3f/3f2d06871f14bead2e13d9fcb373560dcd74b30f74c3d8f9a6319762130ec162.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(-3)=-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101284 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da3b0feb-e320-535c-8435-9a7c6b6e71cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101284 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-6 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4 \\\\  & =-10 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4+2 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-6,-10,-8\\}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101652 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101652 #edb/ref 1061 1572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101652 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2b6e44b-f469-543a-a1ac-99aecca54d2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101652 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101655 #edb/ref 1061 1573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101655 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "adca67a5-ec48-5624-857c-3dfe325add06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101655 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101656 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101656 #edb/ref 1061 1577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101656 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "571b8ef9-5e66-53a6-b671-21d8c331aca2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101656 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101814 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101814 #edb/ref 1061 37716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101814 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a96fc6c3-1e3a-55cd-a97a-a9adb0305a19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101814 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102025 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will learn how to use the graph of a function's derivative to maximize the function. Let's understand how this process works by studying a specific example.\n\nThe graph of $y = f'(x),$ the *derivative* of $f(x),$ is shown in the picture below. \n\n![](images/1e/1ef0f93de1825106880b3fdcd3c704c72e0748df277e5098064d6ee9abc9bdf8.png)\n\nThe areas of the shaded regions are indicated on the diagram. Additionally, it is known that $f(-2)=3.$ Our goal is find the *maximum* value of $f(x)$ on the interval $x\\in [-2,2].$\n\nTo find the maximum value of $f(x),$ we use the candidates test.\n\nAccording to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. Therefore, we proceed as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ so there are no non-stationary critical points. Additionally, we see that $f'(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are \n\n$$\nx=-2,\\quad x=-1,\\quad x=2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n- The value of $f(x)$ at the left endpoint is given:\n\n$$\nf(-2) = 3\n$$\n\n- To compute $f(-1),$ we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus. Since the area of the left triangle in our picture equals ${\\color{red}{1}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To compute $f(2),$ we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus once more. Using the fact that the area of the left triangle equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$ and the area of the right triangle equals ${\\color{blue}{\\dfrac32}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+1-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we *subtracted* the area of the right triangle since it lies *under* the $x$-axis.\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\left\\lbrace 3, 4, \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right\\rbrace = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102025 #edb/ref 1065 819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102025 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0533716e-c304-5c45-8aa3-f51853f120c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102025 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510787 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510787 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17