=g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ g(5) & =g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(4-6) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,6,0\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511474 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511474 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343704" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511474 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511474 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a3/a3f57d0658f89f37df9a838b4a79cb9d4ed3d4183187013923c26fb3bd836e10.png)\n\nThe function $k(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,7].$ The graph of ${k}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $k(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4,$ $7,$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $k(x)$ on the interval $[-2,7]$ given that $k(-2)=2.$\n\n${k}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511474 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27335de7-ab94-5f77-a70c-95b3aeec92aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511474 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $k(x)$ on $[-2,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${k}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,7),$ and ${k}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1,5.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,5,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $k$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-2) & =2 \\\\ k(1) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 