}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+7.5 \\\\  & =5.5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(7.5-1) \\\\  & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,5.5,4.5\\}=5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511488 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d7/d7aa23647a61b4d13e9468eafc34947e8b01046a31d67da8ee997476e13dec95.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $2\\pi .$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-3\\pi .$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47b1ac6a-9da0-5717-9e95-4b0413ae2c6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511488 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-3\\pi  \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\pi -2\\pi  \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3\\pi ,-5\\pi \\}=-3\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-3\\pi ,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511489 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d8/d8500caba027f22623c3e20287f92f12e56aad667788708189f68205f4122195.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,9].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4,$ $8,$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,9]$ given that $f(0)=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "488dbbc3-e305-587b-8387-bc6f8dfc6d19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511489 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3,7.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,7,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(7) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{7}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(9) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{9}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8+2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,5,-3,-1\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511497 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/aa/aa70775eb1b2968406c61e1bebad52139f492890e71a9977582fc1898ba5ee8f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $9.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55621ada-4f38-5ee1-82ac-26e8704cbd38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511497 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\n We see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-9 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,-7\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511529 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511529 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15744" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511529 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511529 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/39/392562d7675b8ebddd33cbdea448481654f9e643b088b949d4337618517c537b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,5]$ given that $f(0)=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511529 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "907c4d01-952e-54f5-819a-03c35c5b1210" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511529 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,1,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/82/82f6ace89a1d880390b5f6c2dbe344a6230d5bc91ba11521ac996b5e59ebc49c.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(1) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,\\frac{11}{2},4,5\\}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{11}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511537 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511537 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511537 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511537 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/3c/3cb97b20d164c2976ae046f83edbbb499514ff3c7ddfbfa9b703918457eba467.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,6]$ given that $f(-4)=7.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511537 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a52ab505-f7f2-54ed-afb3-b43bed5cfef5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511537 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2$ and $x=3.$  Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of  $f(x)$  for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/e6/e67e51128fe4392e814eec6d9d6ab17d7d7718af38fd556a0a47c603174e9dcd.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =7, \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =7+2 \\\\  & =9, \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =9+15 \\\\  & =24, \\\\ f(6) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =24-4.5 \\\\  & =19.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{7,9,24,19.5\\}=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=24,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511553 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511553 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511553 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511553 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/86/8678ae0607275abf4732943add33116314569efc3afb44e92a15d6e78eeacd64.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ if $f(-4)=-5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511553 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4376358-d419-5ca5-9d5f-60ee48a180a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511553 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of one square, two trapezoids, and one triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/7f/7fe0ee64fd29812d2ee536d7285f5e4674d8985a2dc1e27bf230dafb0159046e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-5 \\\\ f(1) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5+(1+5+4) \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-5,5,2\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511565 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511565 #edb/ref 1069 "q-316990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511565 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511565 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d4/d4077eba1972d32e7ab9b7bfbe550654395412076f175f48b1d78a49a21641ae.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[0,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ if $f(0)=5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511565 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de089661-beea-5ee0-bfa5-18ccd2aea3c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511565 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of two trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/aa/aade60ca0b004cad7f0bb1e2de4e5ea354e1f87625b3d915e745381c489c41a4.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5 \\\\  & =7.5 \\\\ f(6) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5-4 \\\\  & =3.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{5,7.5,3.5\\}=7.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=7.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511569 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511569 #edb/ref 1069 "q-144833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511569 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511569 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/56/567044329a721ac622d2f66e675577e202d65d3de145a93fa430edfb31ced4b1.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,4]$ given that $f(-2)=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e22ec0ee-78ef-534d-a2b2-dbeef1b12436" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511569 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,4.$  \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =3 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3,3\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186511378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511577 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511577 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511577 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/82/82a08c804381b11ae118691d82c4801b655cd8e0b131677f67ed6e63b8400d7c.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[2,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $13.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[2,6]$ given that $f(2)=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511577 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f1f17c93-4d26-549b-8492-2ec95bf66e77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186511577 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[2,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (2,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are$x=2,\\,6.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =-2 \\\\ f(6) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2+13 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,11\\}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=11,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
